Chapter: E1000AsdeArms
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Kernel chapter Pci
  cites Dev chapter NicAsde

 The ASDE stage that is going to the board, run against the emulator so
 the arms are known to execute before a human is spent on them.

 This CANNOT answer the question the stage exists to ask. The model's
 STATUS.LU does not consult CTRL.ASDE at all, so both arms come back the
 same here by construction, and that agreement is worth exactly nothing
 as evidence about the I219. What it does establish is that the stage
 compiles, that both arms run to completion against a device, that the
 second arm's bring-up is not wedged by the first, and that the rendered
 line is well formed. A payload whose stage faults at the bench has
 spent a trip to discover a compile-time fact.

Section: Judges

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

 "Ran" is asked as "came back carrying a give-up state I recognise", not
 as "STATUS was nonzero". Those are the same thing only while the bed
 grants a link: under -e1000-no-link STATUS reads zero on an arm that ran
 perfectly, and the earlier version of this judge answered no and named
 itself `both-arms-run` while doing it. A judge whose answer depends on a
 condition it does not name is the defect this stage already paid for once.

  gave-known : NicAsdeArm -> Boolean
  gave-known (a) =
   if (a.na-gave) == na-gave-ok then True
   else if (a.na-gave) == na-gave-reset then True
   else (a.na-gave) == na-gave-nolink

  both-arms-run : PciDevice -> Boolean
  both-arms-run (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in let on = na-bring-up mmio True
   in let off = na-bring-up mmio False
   in gave-known on

 The second arm must not be poisoned by the first. Both reset and bring
 the PHY up from scratch, so the later reading has to be as live as the
 earlier one; a zero here would mean the first arm left the part wedged.

  second-arm-live : PciDevice -> Boolean
  second-arm-live (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in let on = na-bring-up mmio True
   in let off = na-bring-up mmio False
   in gave-known off

 The arms differ in one bit of CTRL and nothing else, so under a model
 that ignores ASDE they must agree. If this ever answers no on the
 EMULATOR, the stage has grown a difference that is not the bit.

  arms-agree-in-bed : PciDevice -> Boolean
  arms-agree-in-bed (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in let on = na-bring-up mmio True
   in let off = na-bring-up mmio False
   in (on.na-status) == (off.na-status)

  line-nonempty : PciDevice -> Boolean
  line-nonempty (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in text-length (na-line "ASDE=1" (na-bring-up mmio True)) > 20

 The give-up state is the row that flew and did not paint. Under the model
 the link comes up, so this reads ok; the arm that matters is the one
 below, which asks whether a state that is NOT ok can render at all.

  gave-is-ok-here : PciDevice -> Boolean
  gave-is-ok-here (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in (na-bring-up mmio True).na-gave == na-gave-ok

 A hand-built arm in each give-up state, so the text for a state this bed
 cannot produce is still known to render rather than being assumed. The
 flight that painted nothing is what this arm exists for.

  refused : NicAsdeArm
  refused = NicAsdeArm { na-status = 0, na-aneg = 0 - 1,
                         na-gave = na-gave-reset, na-phy = 0 }

  nolink : NicAsdeArm
  nolink = NicAsdeArm { na-status = 0, na-aneg = 0,
                        na-gave = na-gave-nolink, na-phy = 1 }

  give-up-rows-render : Boolean
  give-up-rows-render =
   if text-length (na-line "ASDE=1" refused) <= 20 then False
   else text-length (na-line "ASDE=0" nolink) > 20

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let scan = pci-scan-bus 0
    in act
      when e1000-find scan
        is Just (dev) -> act
          print-line-uni ("first arm runs  : " & yn (both-arms-run dev))
          print-line-uni ("second arm live : " & yn (second-arm-live dev))
          print-line-uni ("arms agree      : " & yn (arms-agree-in-bed dev))
          print-line-uni ("line renders    : " & yn (line-nonempty dev))
          print-line-uni ("gave is ok here : " & yn (gave-is-ok-here dev))
          print-line-uni ("give-up renders : " & yn give-up-rows-render)
        end
        is None -> print-line-uni ("candidate found : no")
    end
  end

